From 7b47c9d9fa275383139cf4b4bd2a4bde9a12f962 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:53:42 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 39/56] super-amd: keep the state of volumes in mdmon

RAID1, RAID10 and RAID5 volumes (they have an AMD write-intent bitmap)
and RAIDABLE ones (it has none) now run read-write: mdmon keeps their
state the way the AMD driver does.

- dirty/clean in the write-intent bitmap on element [0][0]: the whole
  volume is marked while md has it active, so that the AMD driver checks
  it after a crash under Linux; after a crash under either, md resyncs
  from the first bit set;
- a shadow of that bitmap, how mdmon last wrote it, in the sector after
  the md bitmap header on the same disk, which neither the kernel nor
  the AMD firmware and drivers write: rcraid.sys 9.5.0.201 clears the
  bitmap a crash under Linux left dirty without checking the volume,
  and 9.3.3.218 checks the mirrors but not RAID5 parity (seen with both
  in a Windows VM).  A shadow still dirty over a clean bitmap means the
  AMD driver cleared it: md resyncs from the bit the shadow is dirty
  from, as after a crash, and --examine says so;
- a member md fails is recorded as failed (element flags 0x10), a member
  missing when the volume goes into use, or when an mdmon takes the
  running volume over, as offline (0x30) - so is one the AMD driver left
  with 0x20 alone, as its first write would -, and the volume CRITICAL,
  in a new configuration: the packet goes to the next slot of the ring
  of every up-to-date disk, then the commit blocks with the
  generation bumped; disks that are missing or failing are left alone
  and stay stale, and so does a member out of date (its generation
  keeps it out of the volume), while a disk that is here and holds no
  part of a volume in use - free, a spare, a member failed out - gets
  every configuration, and the live one when it is behind, as the AMD
  driver writes its configuration to every disk it finds
  (RC_WriteAllConfigData);
- a member failure is kept to its volume (fail_subarray_only, earlier in
  this series), as the AMD driver keeps it: the disk stays in service in
  its other volumes and gets the configurations (seen with rcraid 9.3.3
  in a QEMU guest: a write error failed the RAID5 element alone, a RAID1
  on the same disk stayed normal, the PD online); a disk failed in every
  volume it served is written to no more, its PD record OFFLINE, as is
  one a configuration write failed on;
- a disk that does not take a transform log is failed in md, as the AMD
  driver fails its element, if md runs the volume on without it: the
  log is on the others, and no one reads the copy left behind;
- a disk of the set that comes after mdmon has taken over a
  configuration of another controller, with that very packet, gets the
  configurations as the others do: the AMD driver takes the configuration
  last online with a disk over a foreign one, and left so it would go
  back to that packet for the whole set, the disks mdmon wrote missing
  from it (seen with the AMD Linux driver, rcraid 9.3.3, in a QEMU
  guest); after a restart of mdmon the packet taken over is found again
  in the ring of a disk with the live configuration;
- a disk that missed a configuration while md ran it in sync in every
  volume it holds (-I per disk: managemon took the array in before the
  disk) catches up with the live one, as an idle disk does;
- the initialization (CREATE_REDUNDANCY) of a volume made in the BIOS:
  md resyncs it, its progress goes to the AMD transform log - a reboot,
  into Linux or the AMD driver, goes on from there - and its end into
  the configuration; so does a check (CHECK_REDUNDANCY) the AMD driver
  left unfinished, a Verify in Windows cut short: md's resync does what
  that check does, and more (RAID5 parity);
- a disk that joins the running container (udev adds them one at a
  time) or leaves it is taken in or dropped by the monitor.

A volume with another transform under way (a rebuild, a migration)
stays read-only.  mdmon's two threads share the metadata: one of them
at a time works on it.  A write that keeps failing - of the
configuration, a transform log, the bitmap - is told in the kernel log
once, and once more when it goes through again.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.2, AI models by Anthropic.
---
 super-amd.c | 2425 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 2410 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

